---
import { calculateChoReset, type ChoReset } from "../lib/alignment-engine";
import type { Mode } from "../lib/site-data";
interface Props { scenario: ChoReset; mode: Mode }
const { scenario, mode } = Astro.props;
const result = calculateChoReset(scenario);
const decimals = mode === "beginner" ? 3 : 6;
const f = (value: number) => value.toFixed(decimals);
const resetState = scenario.previousState.map((v, i) => v * result.reset[i]!);
const matrixState = scenario.recurrentMatrix.map((row) => row.reduce((sum, v, i) => sum + v * scenario.previousState[i]!, 0));
---
<section class="mass-ledger alignment-ledger" aria-labelledby="cho-reset-heading">
  <header class="ledger-header"><div><p class="eyebrow">Synthetic example · one encoder update</p><h2 id="cho-reset-heading">Where does reset act?</h2></div><p class="ledger-folio">Main Eq. 8<br />Attachment §1.1</p></header>
  <div class="ledger-prompt"><p><strong>{mode === "beginner" ? "Predict first." : "Audit first."}</strong> {mode === "beginner" ? "Can moving a coordinatewise gate across a mixing matrix change the result?" : "Reconstruct reset-before-matrix and reset-after-matrix. Which contributions remain equal, and which source fixes z’s role?"}</p></div>
  <div class="trace-intro">
    <p>Hold the previous state, external term, matrix and gates fixed. Only reset placement changes between the two calculations. The main paper applies reset before the recurrent matrix; the attachment’s encoder applies it after.</p>
    <p class="provenance-note mt-3">All values are authored. Gate logits are supplied combined inputs to sigmoid, not calculated from learned gate matrices. The external term absorbs input and bias. This is a two-coordinate encoder comparison, independent of the phrase-score example above; it does not reconstruct either paper’s trained run or the attachment decoder.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="trace-history">
    <table class="trace-table alignment-coordinates"><caption>Shared synthetic state and gate inputs · {decimals} decimals</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Quantity</th><th scope="col">Coord. 1</th><th scope="col">Coord. 2</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {[
          ["Previous state", scenario.previousState], ["External term", scenario.externalTerm],
          ["Reset gate r", result.reset], ["Update gate z", result.update],
        ].map(([label, values]) => <tr><th scope="row">{label}</th>{(values as number[]).map((value) => <td>{f(value)}</td>)}</tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="mt-3">Mixing matrix U: first row [1, 2]; second row [−0.5, 1]. Each row combines both previous-state coordinates. Reset scales what enters the candidate computation. Update z weights the old state in the final mixture.</p>
    <details class="trace-arithmetic"><summary>Inspect gate inputs and matrix order</summary>
      <table class="trace-table alignment-coordinates mt-3"><caption>Supplied gate logits and reset-order intermediates</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Quantity</th><th scope="col">Coord. 1</th><th scope="col">Coord. 2</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{[["Reset logit → sigmoid", scenario.resetLogits], ["Update logit → sigmoid", scenario.updateLogits], ["r × previous state", resetState], ["U × previous state", matrixState]].map(([label, values]) => <tr><th scope="row">{label}</th>{(values as number[]).map((value) => <td>{f(value)}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="mt-3">For the main formula’s first recurrent coordinate: 1 × 0.15 + 2 × (−0.30) = −0.45. For the attachment encoder: 0.25 × (1 × 0.6 + 2 × (−0.4)) = −0.05. Unequal reset values and off-diagonal matrix entries expose a difference that a scalar example could hide.</p>
    </details>
  </div>
  {(["main", "attachment"] as const).map((variant) => {
    const trace = result[variant];
    return <div class="trace-history">
      <h3>{variant === "main" ? "Main paper · reset, then mix" : "Attachment encoder · mix, then reset"}</h3>
      <table class="trace-table alignment-coordinates"><caption>{variant === "main" ? "Main Eq. 8" : "Attachment encoder"} · complete synthetic update</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Operation</th><th scope="col">Coord. 1</th><th scope="col">Coord. 2</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {[["Recurrent term", trace.recurrentTerm], ["+ external term", trace.preactivation], ["tanh → candidate", trace.candidate], ["z × old state", trace.oldContribution], ["(1 − z) × candidate", trace.candidateContribution], ["Sum → new state", trace.state]].map(([label, values], index) => <tr class:list={[{ "trace-result": index === 5 }]}><th scope="row">{label}</th>{(values as number[]).map((value) => <td>{f(value)}</td>)}</tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>;
  })}
  <div class="trace-comparison">
    <details class="prediction-reveal"><summary>Check reset and carry</summary>
      <p>The formulas differ here even though all supplied inputs match. Reset controls the candidate’s use of old state; it does not directly erase the old-state contribution. In coordinate 1, z = 0.8 keeps a 0.48 old-state contribution in both formulas.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">At the idealized limit z = 1 the mixture keeps the old coordinate; at z = 0 it uses the candidate. Reset r = 0 removes that coordinate’s candidate input before mixing in the main formula. These limits explain the equation; finite sigmoid inputs do not produce exact zero or one.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">This arithmetic cannot establish which formula was used in the historical run, or that one gate caused a BLEU gain. The two-coordinate state is neither a word-probability distribution nor an LSTM cell.</p>
    </details>
    <p class="provenance-note mt-3">Calculations use unrounded values; display rounding never feeds another operation. The official main and attachment remain separate sources.</p>
  </div>
</section>
